    <date year="2026" month="November" day="19"/>
    <abstract>
      <?line 13?>

<t>A fixture, not a specification: base 64 encoding as nish's std base64
writes it, into a caller's buffer from an index, answering the index just
past the last character. The interface declares that (out, at) -&gt; end
write as a bytes result.</t>
    </abstract>
  </front>
  <middle>
    <?line 20?>

<section anchor="introduction">
      <name>Introduction</name>
      <t>The key words "<bcp14>MUST</bcp14>", "<bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>REQUIRED</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHALL</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHALL
NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHOULD</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHOULD NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>RECOMMENDED</bcp14>", "<bcp14>NOT RECOMMENDED</bcp14>",
"<bcp14>MAY</bcp14>", and "<bcp14>OPTIONAL</bcp14>" in this document are to be interpreted as
described in BCP 14 <xref target="RFC2119"/> <xref target="RFC8174"/> when, and only when, they
appear in all capitals, as shown here.</t>
      <?line -18?>

<t>The module is nish's own nish/base64, checked and run with <tt>--nish-root</tt>.
Its tests in tests/spec/emit run the same emitted test against copies of it
that write one byte wrong, past the end, or answer the wrong end, which the
contract refuses: the test passes the buffer at a nonzero index, between
guard bytes, and checks every byte outside the written range.</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="interface">
      <name>Interface</name>
      <sourcecode type="ts-interface"><![CDATA[
// module: nish/base64
export const base64EncodeInto = (src: u8[] /* window */, dst: u8[] /* out 2 * src.length + 4 */): u8[]
]]></sourcecode>
    </section>
    <section anchor="encoding">
      <name>Encoding</name>
      <t anchor="R-B64-ENCODE">base64EncodeInto <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> write the padded base 64 of the window's bytes, in the
standard alphabet, and only those characters, from the index it is given.</t>
      <t anchor="R-B64-END">base64EncodeInto <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> answer the index just past the last character it
wrote.</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="security-considerations">
      <name>Security Considerations</name>
      <t>Base 64 is an encoding, not a cipher: it hides nothing.</t>
    </section>
